**assistant**:
> Two things are going on here:
> 
> 1. **What `TIFFGetField` actually returns for these tags**, and  
> 2. **The specific TIFF you’re now using probably doesn’t have tiepoints at all.**
> 
> Let’s unpack both.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 1. `TIFFGetField` and GEO tags
> 
> For the GeoTIFF “model” tags:
> 
> - `TIFFTAG_GEOTIEPOINTS` (tag 33922)  
> - `TIFFTAG_GEOPIXELSCALE` (tag 33550)
> 
> `TIFFGetField` usually behaves like this:
> 
> ```c++
> int nTieDoubles = 0;
> double* tiePoints = nullptr;
> if (TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOTIEPOINTS, &nTieDoubles, &tiePoints)) {
>     // nTieDoubles = number of doubles (must be multiple of 6)
> }
> ```
> 
> and
> 
> ```c++
> int nScaleDoubles = 0;
> double* pixelScale = nullptr;
> if (TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOPIXELSCALE, &nScaleDoubles, &pixelScale)) {
>     // nScaleDoubles = number of doubles (should be 3: scaleX, scaleY, scaleZ)
> }
> ```
> 
> Key points:
> 
> - The **second argument** returned (`nTieDoubles`, `nScaleDoubles`) is **count of doubles**, not “number of tie points” or “number of triplets”.
>   - For tie points: `nTieDoubles = 6 * nTiePoints` (each tie point is 6 doubles).
>   - For pixel scale: `nScaleDoubles` is usually 3 (scaleX, scaleY, scaleZ).
> - If `TIFFGetField` **returns 0**, the tag is not present.
> 
> Your current output:
> 
> ```text
> countTiePoints = 0
> countPixelScale = 3
> ```
> 
> means:
> 
> - The file has **no GEOTIEPOINTS tag** at all (or `TIFFGetField` failed to retrieve it).
> - The file has **GEOPIXELSCALE with 3 doubles**, which is perfectly normal.
> 
> So your message “No usable geotransform in GeoTIFF” is coming from *your* logic, not from libtiff; you’re presumably requiring both pixel scale and tie points.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 2. Why did it work before and not now?
> 
> Likely explanations:
> 
> 1. **You’ve switched to a different TIFF**  
>    Many GeoTIFFs store the geotransform using:
>    - `ModelPixelScaleTag` (GEOPIXELSCALE) **and**
>    - `ModelTiepointTag` (GEOTIEPOINTS)
> 
>    But some store only pixel scale + corner coordinates via **ModelTransformationTag (33920)**, or use different combinations.
> 
>    Your “working” file probably had:
>    - GEOTIEPOINTS: count ≥ 6  
>    - GEOPIXELSCALE: count = 3  
> 
>    Your new file seems to have:
>    - GEOTIEPOINTS: not present  
>    - GEOPIXELSCALE: present, count = 3  
> 
> 2. **You might be misinterpreting `countTiePoints`**  
>    If you previously treated `countTiePoints` as “number of tie points” rather than “number of doubles”, older code might have “worked by accident” when count was 6 and you only needed the first one.
> 
> 3. **Different writer / GDAL version / export settings**  
>    A different tool (or different GDAL options) may have written a file without tie points, relying only on other metadata.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 3. How to robustly get a geotransform
> 
> You should:
> 
> 1. **Check the return value of `TIFFGetField`**, not just the count:
> 
> ```c++
> int tieCount = 0;
> double* tiePoints = nullptr;
> 
> if (TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOTIEPOINTS, &tieCount, &tiePoints) && tieCount >= 6) {
>     // At least one tiepoint: 6 doubles
>     int nTiePoints = tieCount / 6;
> } else {
>     // No tiepoints
> }
> ```
> 
> 2. **Use pixel scale + tiepoint when available**:
> 
> Common pattern:
> 
> - You want a GeoTransform:  
>   `GT[0] = originX`  
>   `GT[1] = pixelWidth`  
>   `GT[2] = 0`  
>   `GT[3] = originY`  
>   `GT[4] = 0`  
>   `GT[5] = -pixelHeight` (if north-up)
> 
> Example:
> 
> ```c++
> double* pixelScale = nullptr;
> int scaleCount = 0;
> double* tiePoints = nullptr;
> int tieCount = 0;
> 
> bool hasScale = TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOPIXELSCALE, &scaleCount, &pixelScale) && scaleCount >= 2;
> bool hasTie   = TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOTIEPOINTS, &tieCount, &tiePoints) && tieCount >= 6;
> 
> if (hasScale && hasTie) {
>     // Use first tie point
>     double i = tiePoints[0];
>     double j = tiePoints[1];
>     double k = tiePoints[2]; // usually 0
>     double x = tiePoints[3];
>     double y = tiePoints[4];
>     double z = tiePoints[5];
> 
>     double scaleX = pixelScale[0];
>     double scaleY = pixelScale[1];
> 
>     double GT[6];
>     GT[1] = scaleX;
>     GT[5] = -scaleY;   // assuming north-up
>     GT[2] = GT[4] = 0;
> 
>     // If the tiepoint is at pixel (0,0), this is easy:
>     if (i == 0.0 && j == 0.0) {
>         GT[0] = x;
>         GT[3] = y;
>     } else {
>         // more general case: account for offset
>         GT[0] = x - i * scaleX;
>         GT[3] = y - j * (-scaleY);
>     }
> 
>     // you now have a valid GT
> } else {
>     // consider also checking MODELMODELTRANSFORMATIONTAG (33920)
> }
> ```
> 
> 3. **If you only have pixel scale (no tiepoints)**  
> Then you need another source of origin (e.g., ModelTransformationTag, or some external metadata). With only `GEOPIXELSCALE`, you *cannot* fully reconstruct a geotransform; you know pixel size but not where the raster is placed.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 4. What to do now
> 
> To debug your specific case:
> 
> 1. **Print whether the tags exist**:
> 
> ```c++
> int tieCount = 0, scaleCount = 0;
> double *tiePoints = nullptr, *pixelScale = nullptr;
> 
> int hasTie = TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOTIEPOINTS, &tieCount, &tiePoints);
> int hasScale = TIFFGetField(tiff, TIFFTAG_GEOPIXELSCALE, &scaleCount, &pixelScale);
> 
> cout << "hasTie=" << hasTie << " tieCount=" << tieCount << endl;
> cout << "hasScale=" << hasScale << " scaleCount=" << scaleCount << endl;
> ```
> 
> 2. **Run `gdalinfo -mm -proj4 your.tif`** on the file and paste the output (or at least the “Model Tiepoint” / “Model Pixel Scale” lines). That will show exactly which tags are present.
> 
> If you paste a sample `gdalinfo` output here (or the tiff tags dump via `tiffinfo -D`), I can tell you exactly how to reconstruct the transform for that file.
